        q: 'How do you use Confluence in your project, and what do you document?',
        testing: 'Documentation habits and knowledge sharing.',
        answer: `<strong>Sample script:</strong> "Confluence is our shared knowledge base. We keep requirement and design pages, architecture descriptions with diagrams, API contracts, environment and deployment guides, onboarding documentation, meeting notes and decision records. I write and update pages for the features I build, such as how a micro-frontend is configured, how to run it locally and known issues. I link pages to Jira tickets and review pages when the code changes, so they do not become outdated. I also document runbooks for common production problems."<br><br>Mention what you personally wrote, for example a setup guide or an architecture page.`,
        mistake: 'Writing documentation once and never updating it, so it misleads people.',
        followupQ: 'How do you keep documentation accurate?',
        followupA: 'Assign owners, review pages as part of the definition of done for changes, keep them close to the code where possible and remove or archive obsolete pages.',
