        q: 'Explain the Decorator pattern and give a JavaScript or Vue example.',
        testing: 'Adding behavior without modifying the original.',
        answer: `A decorator wraps an object or function to add behavior while keeping the same interface. In JavaScript this is commonly a higher-order function: for example wrapping an API function with logging, caching, retry or timing. HTTP client interceptors that add authentication headers are decorator-like. In Vue, a wrapper component or a composable can add loading handling around another. The benefit is composition without changing the wrapped code, and the cost is that many layers make debugging and stack traces harder, so keep each wrapper single-purpose and document the order.`,
        code: `function withRetry(fn, attempts = 3) {
  return async function (...args) {
    let lastError;
    for (let i = 0; i < attempts; i++) {
      try { return await fn.apply(this, args); } catch (error) { lastError = error; }
    }
    throw lastError;
  };
}
const loadPlans = withRetry(fetchPlans);`,
