        testing: 'Connecting the design pattern with the language feature.',
        answer: `The Proxy pattern places a stand-in object in front of another to control access: for lazy loading, caching, access control, logging or validation. JavaScript has a built-in <code>Proxy</code> that intercepts operations such as get and set. Vue 3 reactive objects are proxies that track reads and trigger updates on writes. You can also write a caching proxy for an expensive service or a validation proxy for configuration objects. Considerations: a proxy is a different identity from its target, interception adds overhead and debugging can be confusing, so use it where the interception brings clear value.`,
