        answer: `An adapter converts one interface into another that the client expects. Typical frontend uses: mapping a backend response into the model the UI needs, so a backend rename affects only the adapter; wrapping a third-party library behind your own interface, which made replacing libraries during the Vue 2 to Vue 3 migration easier; and adapting a legacy service to a new contract. It keeps external details out of components, centralizes validation and makes testing simpler with fixtures. The cost is an extra layer, so apply it at boundaries that change or that you do not control.`,
