        id: 'mfe-why-domains-telecom', difficulty: 'senior', tags: ['micro-frontends', 'domains', 'architecture'],
        q: 'Why did your project use micro-frontends, and how were the domains split?',
        testing: 'Justifying the architecture by organization and domain boundaries, not by fashion.',
        answer: `<strong>Template, adapt to your facts:</strong> "Our telecom platform serves three distinct domains: Self-care, the customer-facing website; Agent, used by support and sales agents; and Retail, used by store staff. Each has different users, release cadence, permissions and host environment: Self-care is a public website built with Module Federation so its features can be released independently, while the Agent application is hosted inside Salesforce. Separate teams owned each domain, so we split the frontend into independently built and deployed micro-frontends with a shared shell, shared design tokens and common services such as authentication. That let teams release without coordinating a single big deployment, and let us migrate Vue 2 to Vue 3 one application at a time. The cost was extra complexity in shared dependencies, routing, communication and testing, which we handled with explicit contracts."<br><br>Be ready to say what you would not split further and where the boundaries came from: business domains, teams and deployment needs.`,
        mistake: 'Saying micro-frontends are always better, or being unable to name a concrete benefit and cost from your own project.',
