        answer: `A common pattern is that the shell owns the top-level routes and maps each domain prefix, such as self-care, agent or retail, to a micro-frontend, while each micro-frontend owns the routes below its prefix. This keeps deep links working and the browser history consistent. Each application should run under a configurable base path so it works both standalone in development and inside the shell. Define how navigation between applications happens, usually through the shell router or a navigation event, and avoid hard-coded URLs. Handle unknown routes, authorization guards and the loading state while a remote is fetched. When the host is a third-party page, routing may need hash mode or messages to the parent.`,
