        id: 'mfe-auth-session-sharing', difficulty: 'advanced', tags: ['authentication', 'session', 'security'],
        q: 'How do you handle authentication and sessions across multiple micro-frontends?',
        testing: 'A single sign-on experience without scattering tokens and duplicating logic.',
        answer: `Authenticate once in the shell or at the identity provider and share the result through a controlled interface instead of each application running its own login. Where possible use secure HTTP-only cookies handled by a backend-for-frontend, so micro-frontends call APIs without ever reading a token. If tokens must live in the browser, expose them through one shared auth service that handles refresh in a single place, so parallel refreshes do not race. Each application should still rely on the backend for authorization and not only on client role checks. Handle logout across applications and tabs, expiry in the middle of work and the different authentication mechanism when an application is hosted inside another platform.`,
