        answer: `Each micro-frontend has its own pipeline that lints, tests, builds and publishes hashed assets to a CDN, then updates a manifest or the remote entry pointer. Keep old assets available so users on an older shell can still load them. Cache the remote entry briefly or revalidate it, and give hashed assets long cache lifetimes. Use backward-compatible contracts and contract tests so a release cannot break the shell, and release gradually with feature flags or canary traffic and fast rollback by pointing the manifest back. Record which version of each micro-frontend is live, and tag errors with that version to find regressions quickly.`,
