        q: 'What does a typical day look like in your project?',
        testing: 'Familiarity with a real development workflow, including meetings, coding, review and testing.',
        answer: `Describe a realistic day in order, and mention the agile ceremonies you actually follow.<br><br><strong>Sample script:</strong> "My day starts around [time]. I check messages, pull requests and any build or production alerts. Then we have a short daily stand-up where I share what I did yesterday, what I plan today and any blockers. After that I focus on my sprint tasks: building or fixing a feature, integrating an API, writing tests and testing it locally against the design. During the day I review teammates' pull requests, answer questions and clarify requirements with the designer, backend developer or product owner. Before raising a pull request I run linting, tests and a self-review. When the build passes and the review is approved, it merges and goes through the pipeline to the test environment where QA verifies it. Once or twice a sprint we have planning, backlog refinement, a demo and a retrospective. In the last part of the day I update my tickets and plan tomorrow."<br><br>Mention tools you use, such as <code>Git</code>, <code>Jira</code>, <code>Slack</code> or <code>Teams</code> and a CI service.`,
