        id: 'deployment-flags-lifecycle', difficulty: 'senior', tags: ['feature-flags', 'kill-switch', 'ownership'],
        q: 'What makes a feature flag a safe operational control rather than permanent branching debt?',
        testing: 'Flag ownership, fallback semantics and removal planning.',
        answer: `Define the owner, expiry date, default behavior and removal condition when introducing the flag. Evaluate security-sensitive eligibility on the server; a browser flag is not authorization. Keep flag evaluation consistent during SSR and hydration, and choose a safe fallback when configuration is unavailable. Test both branches and combinations that interact with shared state. A kill switch should disable risky work quickly without requiring a broken code path to initialize first. Track stale flags and remove dead code after rollout, because indefinitely supported combinations multiply testing and incident complexity.`,
