        q: 'What serving contract keeps HTML, lazy chunks and styles consistent across deployments?',
        testing: 'Asset lifetime and ordering for active browser sessions.',
        answer: `Publish content-hashed assets before exposing HTML that references them. Cache those immutable URLs for a long time, while keeping HTML and release manifests revalidated or short-lived according to the deployment model. Retain prior assets long enough for old tabs and rollback, including chunks imported hours after the initial load. Do not overwrite bytes at an immutable URL. Make the release pointer switch atomic where possible and coordinate service-worker caches with this contract. Test an old open tab navigating after a new release, not just a fresh browser session.`,
        mistake: 'Deleting the previous asset directory immediately because fresh page loads pass.',
        followupQ: 'Is forced reload a complete fix for failed dynamic imports?',
        followupA: 'No. Use a guarded recovery prompt that preserves work, but fix asset retention and serving consistency rather than creating a reload loop.',
