        q: 'What must agree when a history-mode application is deployed under a subpath?',
        testing: 'Coordination of build URLs, router base and server fallback rules.',
        answer: `The asset base, router base and host rewrite rules must describe the same public path. A direct navigation to an application route should return the application shell, while a missing JavaScript asset must remain a real error rather than returning HTML with a success status. Check trailing slash behavior, redirects and service-worker scope as well. Verify using production output under the actual prefix, including deep links and lazy imports. A development server often supplies forgiving fallbacks that hide these mistakes until deployment, so root-path testing alone is insufficient.`,
        mistake: 'Rewriting every missing request to index.html, including missing assets and API paths.',
        followupQ: 'What clue reveals an asset fallback error?',
        followupA: 'A JavaScript request returning text/html, often followed by a module MIME error or an unexpected-token error in the browser console.',
