        id: 'browser-platform-indexeddb-transactions', difficulty: 'advanced', tags: ['indexeddb', 'transactions', 'durability'],
        q: 'Why can an IndexedDB transaction become inactive across an unrelated await?',
        testing: 'Transaction lifetime rather than assuming ordinary async database semantics.',
        answer: `IndexedDB transactions are tied to the event loop and can auto-commit when their queued database work finishes. Waiting for unrelated network or timer work can leave no active transaction when execution resumes. Fetch or compute outside the transaction, then schedule the necessary database requests together and handle transaction completion or abort. Treat a successful individual request differently from successful transaction commit. Keep migrations explicit and test quota failures and interrupted operations. If a library wraps IndexedDB in promises, understand its transaction guarantees rather than assuming any awaited callback stays inside one atomic unit.`,
        mistake: 'Opening a readwrite transaction, awaiting fetch, then expecting the transaction to remain usable.',
        followupQ: 'What belongs in one transaction?',
        followupA: 'Related writes whose invariants must commit together, such as a draft and its pending-operation record, using only the required object stores.',
