        id: 'browser-platform-bfcache-resume', difficulty: 'senior', tags: ['bfcache', 'page-lifecycle', 'freshness'],
        q: 'What must a dashboard reconsider when restored from the back-forward cache?',
        testing: 'Page restoration as distinct from a new mount or reload.',
        answer: `A restored document can resume its prior DOM and JavaScript state without running the usual initialization again. Use lifecycle signals such as <code>pageshow</code> with its persisted flag to reconsider stale server data, session validity and time-dependent UI. Do not indiscriminately reload and lose useful state; revalidate resources according to their freshness requirements. Pause unnecessary work when hidden and resume connections defensively. Avoid relying on unload handlers for critical persistence because delivery is unreliable and such handlers can affect cache eligibility. Test back and forward navigation with an expired session and changed backend data.`,
        mistake: 'Assuming onMounted or DOMContentLoaded runs whenever the user sees the page again.',
