        answer: `A custom directive is for reusable low-level DOM behavior that does not belong in a component, such as autofocus, click-outside detection, intersection-based lazy loading or tooltips attached to an element. In Vue 3 the hooks mirror component lifecycle names: <code>created</code>, <code>beforeMount</code>, <code>mounted</code>, <code>beforeUpdate</code>, <code>updated</code>, <code>beforeUnmount</code> and <code>unmounted</code>. Always remove listeners and observers in the unmount hooks to avoid leaks. Prefer a component or composable when the logic needs state or rendering, and remember that directives do not run during server rendering unless you provide a server-side version.`,
        mistake: 'Adding event listeners in mounted and never removing them in beforeUnmount.',
        followupQ: 'What changed from Vue 2 directive hooks?',
        followupA: 'The hook names were aligned with the component lifecycle: bind became beforeMount, inserted became mounted, componentUpdated became updated and unbind became unmounted.',
