        id: 'vue3-virtual-dom-compiler-optimizations', difficulty: 'advanced', tags: ['virtual-dom', 'compiler', 'performance'],
        q: 'How does Vue 3 rendering work, and what optimizations does its compiler perform?',
        testing: 'Understanding why Vue 3 can be faster than Vue 2 and what you can do to help it.',
        answer: `Templates are compiled into render functions that create a virtual DOM tree, and the renderer patches the real DOM by comparing old and new trees. Vue 3 compiler adds optimizations: static content is hoisted so it is created once; patch flags mark which parts of a node can change, so updates compare only those; block trees track dynamic children so the diff skips static parts; and inline event handlers can be cached. Keys let the diff reuse elements correctly in lists. You help it by using templates instead of hand-written render functions when possible, keeping stable keys, avoiding unnecessary reactivity on large static data and splitting heavy components.`,
