        id: 'vue3-reactivity-proxy-internals', difficulty: 'advanced', tags: ['reactivity', 'proxy', 'internals'],
        q: 'How does Vue 3 reactivity work with Proxy, and what are its pitfalls?',
        testing: 'Explaining tracking and triggering, and the practical consequences.',
        answer: `<code>reactive</code> wraps an object in a Proxy. When an effect, such as a render or a computed value, reads a property, the get trap records a dependency from that property to the active effect. When the property is set, the set trap triggers the effects that depend on it. Dependencies are stored in a structure keyed by target object and property. Nested objects are made reactive lazily when accessed. Because it uses Proxy, adding properties and array index changes are detected, unlike Vue 2. Pitfalls: destructuring a reactive object into plain variables loses reactivity, so use <code>toRefs</code> or <code>storeToRefs</code>; the proxy and the original object are different identities; replacing a whole reactive variable breaks references; and very large data can be expensive, where <code>shallowRef</code> or <code>markRaw</code> help.`,
