        answer: `Global guards such as <code>beforeEach</code> run before every navigation, and you control the outcome by returning a value: nothing or true to continue, false to cancel, or a location to redirect, so the old next callback is optional. Per-route guards and route <code>meta</code> fields such as a required role keep authorization rules declarative, and in-component guards handle leaving an unsaved form. Routes are lazy loaded by assigning a dynamic import as the component, so each route becomes its own chunk. Keep guards fast, avoid duplicating authorization only on the client because the backend must enforce it, and handle failed chunk loads. In a micro-frontend setup, the shell guards shared authentication and each application guards its own domain routes.`,
