        answer: `Offset pagination asks for a position in a result set that may change between requests. If an item is inserted or deleted before the current page, the next offset shifts and the client sees a duplicate or misses an item. A cursor identifies the last item seen, usually by a sort key, so the next request continues after that point regardless of earlier changes. The ordering must be deterministic, which normally means adding a unique tiebreaker such as the ID to the sort key. Treat the cursor as opaque so the server can change its encoding. Cursor pagination does not give random page access or a cheap total count, and an item edited so that its sort key changes can still appear out of place. Choose by the product need: stable infinite feeds favor cursors, while jump-to-page tables may accept offsets with the known trade-off.`,
